    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's helpful. but a lot of people online argue about which one is more \"important\" for lift. is there a way to visualize it so it's easier to remember? like if you had to explain the \"force\" behind it in a more intuitive way?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine a spinning top. As it spins, its axis starts to v-cut out the air it's entering, slowing it down and creating an upward lift force, thanks to Bernoulli's Principle. Now, imagine this spinning top has an equal and opposite reaction force pushing it backwards - that's Newton's Third Law at work.\n\nThe spinning motion (Bernoulli's Principle) moves the air down, creates the pressure difference, and generates lift, while the reaction force (Newton's Third Law) is what pushes the top backwards, leaving the impression that the spin itself is what's generating lift. This unifying visualization can help you grasp the intertwined forces behind lift on an airplane wing.",
      "source": "target",
